# Line endings
#
# The index is already canonically LF — verified: `git add --renormalize .` on a
# clean tree produces no change. The problem was never the committed content, it
# was the checkout: `core.autocrlf=true` writes CRLF into the working copy, while
# Prettier defaults to `endOfLine: "lf"` and `.prettierrc` sets no override. The
# result was that `prettier --check` failed on nearly every file in any Windows
# working copy even though the repository content was correct — a CI failure with
# no visible cause in the diff.
#
# `eol=lf` settles it per-repository and outranks whatever `core.autocrlf` each
# machine happens to have, so the working copy, the index and Prettier all agree.
# Adding this file changes no blob; existing working copies converge as files are
# next checked out.
#
# There are no CRLF-dependent files here: no `.bat` or `.cmd` scripts exist, and
# the two `.ps1` scripts run fine with LF.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary — never normalize, never diff as text.
# Git already detects these by content; declaring them makes the intent explicit
# and removes the dependency on that detection.
*.odt       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
*.ttf       binary
*.eot       binary
*.zip       binary
*.dll       binary
*.exe       binary
*.snk       binary
